WEBVTT

00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:13,620
I have a slight elevation in my tumor marker test.

00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:16,740
Is that a call for alarm?

00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:20,460
It depends on what tumor marker we're talking about.

00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:27,380
So let's say, so PSA is a tumor marker for prostate cancer.

00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:30,540
And if you still have your prostate,

00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:34,980
and you have a slight elevation in your PSA,

00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:38,140
no, that's not a call for alarm.

00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:42,340
If you have had your prostate surgically removed,

00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:45,100
and you have a slight elevation,

00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:46,740
then that is a call for alarm,

00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:50,180
because your PSA, if you've had your prostate removed,

00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:53,540
should be really close to zero.

00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:57,580
So usually it's measured as point five or something,

00:00:57,580 --> 00:01:00,460
your PSA, and if it jumped up to three,

00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:03,020
when you had surgical removal of your prostate,

00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:05,540
then that would be, even though it's only a three point,

00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:07,540
to a half point jump.

00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:09,420
Good to say, that's nothing,

00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:11,780
but it is, if you don't have a prostate.

00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:15,540
Any of the other tumor markers, the CA9T9,

00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,940
this CA153, 2729,

00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:24,940
common ones would not be a cause of a alarm

00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:27,020
if there's a slight elevation.

00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:31,620
Now, it is good to look at any blood test over time.

00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:34,020
So you're looking at, out of graph,

00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:36,820
if you're getting this slow elevation

00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:38,540
over the last three years,

00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:43,540
it's going in a upward direction on each test.

00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:47,660
Of course, that you need to look at that as a cause of a alarm.

00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:49,300
Remember, with any blood test,

00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,500
it's a clue of something that's going on.

00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:55,100
You don't treat totally based upon a blood test,

00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,260
that's certainly not based upon one blood test.

00:01:57,260 --> 00:02:02,260
So if there is a giant jump in any blood test,

00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,380
so this is not even just a tumor marker test,

00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:10,500
but if there's a giant jump in any of your markers,

00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:13,420
your alkaline phosphatacer, your AST, your ALT,

00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:18,220
or your giant jump in white blood cell count,

00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:23,180
that's always an indication of something possibly going on.

00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:25,820
And you want to take a closer look at that.

00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:28,580
But if let's say you have a CA153,

00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:30,860
I think the normal is zero to 35,

00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:33,900
and let's say it was 42,

00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:35,500
and then we did the test again.

00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:36,980
It was 48.

00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:38,820
Is that a cause for alarm?

00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:41,820
No, but you would also look at the possibility to rerun that again,

00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:51,140
and test it over time.

00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:54,460
So any lab test that you're running,

00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:56,380
any marker that you're looking at

00:02:56,380 --> 00:02:58,540
is a clue to something going on.

00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:01,500
So if you have an elevation or a drop in anything,

00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:05,420
you want to investigate possible reasons why,

00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:09,580
and there could be a lot of very done,

00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:12,500
pathological reasons why things can change.

